+# On-call runbook
+
+For whoever holds the pager for tally. Read it before your first shift.
+
+## Before your shift
+
+- Pages arrive in **Beacon** (blue owl icon). Install the app and log in with SSO before your first shift.
+- The command line tool is `tallyctl`. Its old name, `tally-cli`, still works as an alias for one more release and will
+  then be removed, so use `tallyctl` in everything you type and script.
+- The service listens on port **8731**. Port 8080 is closed now, so anything still pointing at it fails with a refused
+  connection.
+- During an incident, write down the commands you run as you run them. Reconstructing them afterwards is slow.
+
+## Escalation path
+
+| Step | Who gets paged | When |
+|------|----------------|------|
+| 1 | Primary on-call | Immediately |
+| 2 | Secondary on-call | After 15 minutes without acknowledgement from the primary |
+| 3 | Engineering manager (currently Mara) | After 15 more minutes without acknowledgement from the secondary |
+
+The manager is a real step, not a courtesy. The wait is 15 minutes; the earlier 30-minute rule is dropped. Acknowledging
+is just a tap on the phone, so acknowledge first and get to a laptop afterwards.
+
+**Security issues use a separate path.** Don't go through the secondary. Page the security lead directly.
+
+## When to page at night
+
+Between **22:00 and 07:00**, the pager is used only for **severity 1**: customer-facing data loss or a full outage.
+
+**Severity 2** issues, such as a slow dashboard or a delayed batch, don't page at night. They wait for business hours,
+and the primary picks them up in the morning. This rule covers the pager only. It doesn't mean you can ignore the
+dashboards.
+
+## Restarting services
+
+There are four services: `api`, `ingest`, `worker` and `scheduler`. Restart them one at a time:
+
+```
+tallyctl restart api
+tallyctl restart ingest --drain
+tallyctl restart worker
+tallyctl restart scheduler
+```
+
+- `--drain` works only for ingest. It finishes in-flight work, parks a stuck batch in the dead-letter table and then
+  starts the consumer clean. This is the gentler restart meant for ingest.
+- Restarting the API briefly drops open connections.
+- `--timeout SECONDS` gives up after that many seconds (default 120).
+
+Use these to check state and logs before and after a restart:
+
+```
+tallyctl status --watch
+tallyctl logs tail --follow --service ingest
+```
+
+Green in `status` means the parts answer. It doesn't mean the numbers are right.
+
+## Known failure modes
+
+### Ingest queue stuck
+
+**Symptoms:** The queue depth alert fires, or the depth keeps growing. The ingest workers look healthy, but the oldest
+message has been sitting in the queue for hours. A malformed batch has wedged the consumer, which keeps retrying it.
+
+**Fix:** Run `tallyctl restart ingest --drain` first and look for the cause afterwards. A plain restart doesn't help,
+because the consumer picks up the same poisoned batch again. In August the queue emptied six minutes after the drain
+restart. The network wasn't the cause, so don't spend the first half hour there. Inspect the parked batch the next day.
+The permanent fix for the consumer is still in review.
+
+### Database host disk filling up
+
+**Symptoms:** The disk alert fires at 80 percent. At around 97 percent, the database starts refusing writes.
+
+**Fix:** Run these two commands, in this order:
+
+```
+tallyctl db vacuum --online
+tallyctl logs rotate
+```
+
+Vacuum first, because it needs a little scratch space and log rotation doesn't. Use only `--online` in production. A
+plain vacuum locks the tables and makes things worse while ingest is writing. `--full` needs the service stopped. Log
+rotation compresses old files and removes those past retention (`--keep N`, default 14 files). This sequence has worked
+twice: 97 to 61 percent, and 85 to 55 percent. Clearing the cache doesn't free anything, so don't bother.
+
+### API certificate expired
+
+**Symptoms:** The public API answers with certificate errors, and browsers and clients refuse to connect. The external
+synthetic check is usually the first signal. In September, time went into the load balancer, DNS and the last deploy
+before anyone read the expiry date.
+
+**Fix:** Renewing takes two steps. The restart is not optional, because the old certificate keeps being served until
+the API restarts.
+
+```
+tallyctl certs list
+tallyctl certs renew api
+tallyctl restart api
+```
+
+`certs list` shows expiry dates. `certs renew` accepts `api` or `ingest` and supports `--dry-run`. The renewal job now
+raises an alert when it fails. A calendar reminder goes out fourteen days before each expiry. There is no alert on days
+left yet.
+
+### Refused connection on port 8080
+
+**Symptoms:** An old script, health check or bookmark gets a refused connection.
+
+**Fix:** Point it at port 8731. The September release moved the service to 8731 and closed 8080.
+
+### "Unknown subcommand" when purging the cache
+
+**Symptoms:** The old purge command (`purge-cache`) fails with an unknown-subcommand error. It was removed because it
+sometimes left half of the cache in place.
+
+**Fix:** Use `tallyctl cache clear`. It clears all regions by default, or one region with `--region NAME`. A stale chart
+for one region and a correct one for another is a cache question before it's anything else.
